oing backwards. The decided value at the index is preserved and the new leader re-decides after its accept quorum acknowledges, so this is a decision-visibility and consumption defect, not a divergence of decided values, and the regression window is bounded by one accept round.\n---\n030f3832d93a40fd9b111b694d9096e6-interpretation-0\nThis experiment is a variant of the earlier delayed-Prepare probe and is interpreted against its own receipt; the earlier witness remains in its own interpretation. In receipt 81fa48ede3814e47beddc84938133990 (exit 0, test passed, comparison regressed eq true complete and holds on event 1) event 0 again establishes the premise: leader 1 decided 5, node 2 decided 4 with accepted 5, node 5 decided 5 with index 4 reading decided. The new leader's Prepare to node 5 is the only lost message (lost_prepare_count 1); node 5 stays on the cut leader until node5.reconnected(2), after which the queue shows the PrepareReq, the Prepare, node 5's Promise, the AcceptSync to node 5 and node 5's Accepted reply, all flags true. The AcceptSync carries the new leader's decided 4 (event 1 decided_leader2=4), and event 1 reports node 5 at decided 4 with entry_status undecided and regressed true; event 2 reports it back at 5/decided after the new leader re-decides. This matches the source path: the leader's decided index is the maximum among prepare-quorum promises, so the reconnecting follower's later Promise is answered with a lower decided index and sync_log assigns it unconditionally. The consequence is that a decided entry's status is not stable: an application applying Decided entries as it observes them can apply the same entry twice, a client polling get_decided_idx observes it decreasing, and read_decided_suffix shrinks. Values never diverge and the new leader re-decides after its accept quorum acknowledges, so the claim is a decision-visibility and consumption defect under a single lost message plus normal reconnect handling, not a violation of value